<p>We would like to take this opportunity to welcome you to group counselling and provide you with some information that may provide clarity in this process. My name is <strong>Joleen Poh Ee Bing</strong> and I am currently a trainee counsellor at Open University Malaysia.</p>
<p>This informed consent form is designed to ensure that you fully understand the group counselling process and your rights as a participant.</p>

<h2 class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase text-gray-700">Group Counselling Overview</h2>
<p>Group counselling provides a supportive environment where individuals can share experiences, gain insights, and work toward personal growth with the facilitation of a counsellor.</p>
<p>The purpose of group counselling is mutual support and shared growth. Sessions may focus on improving interpersonal skills, emotional wellbeing, and other group-specific goals.</p>
<p>Sessions will occur on a weekly basis for 6 sessions. Each session will take 120 minutes. Participants are expected to adhere to group rules for productive discussions.</p>

<h2 class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase text-gray-700">Participant Rights &amp; Responsibilities</h2>
<p>Group counselling provides a safe, nonjudgmental space to share your thoughts and feelings. You may express concerns or provide feedback to the group counsellor.</p>
<p>As a participant in the group counselling process, you have a responsibility to respect the confidentiality of other members, attend sessions regularly and on time, and engage with others in a respectful and constructive manner.</p>
<p>Participation in group counselling is entirely voluntary. You have the right to withdraw from the group at any time. If you choose to leave, please notify the group leader beforehand.</p>

<h2 class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase text-gray-700">Confidentiality</h2>
<p>Counselling sessions are strictly confidential. Group members must maintain confidentiality regarding what is shared in sessions and as the group leader, I am ethically bound to keep anything we discuss private. However, there are legal and ethical exceptions to this confidentiality, which include:</p>
<ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li><strong>Harm to self or others:</strong> If you pose a danger to yourself or others, I may need to take necessary steps to ensure safety.</li>
    <li><strong>Court order:</strong> In certain legal situations, I may be required to disclose information as mandated by a court order.</li>
    <li><strong>Client&rsquo;s request:</strong> If you request that I share specific information with another individual or entity, I will do so only with your written consent.</li>
    <li><strong>Consultation:</strong> For professional development and supervision purposes, I may discuss our sessions with my supervisor.</li>
</ol>

<h2 class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase text-gray-700">Recording and Observation</h2>
<p>By signing this form, you have given permission to the counsellor to record the counselling session in the form of audio or video. You also grant permission to the counsellor&rsquo;s supervisor and participants of the counselling training program to perform observations on the recorded counselling session conducted for the purpose of supervision and education.</p>

<h2 class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase text-gray-700">Acknowledgement</h2>
<p>I acknowledge that I have read and understand <strong>ALL</strong> the above information and I am fully aware of my rights. I am also aware of limits to confidentiality. If I have any questions or concerns about any of this information, I agree to discuss these concerns with the counsellor.</p>
